---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BoxUtilizationCalculator from '../../components/BoxUtilizationCalculator';
import '../../styles/packmetry-workspace.css';
import '../../styles/box-utilization-tool.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Box Utilization Calculator — Packmetry"
  description="Compare identical item volume with a carton's internal volume, calculate utilization and remaining volume, and understand why volume alone does not prove packing fit."
>
  <main class="pm-utilization-page">
    <div class="pm-utilization-shell">
      <header class="pm-utilization-hero">
        <p class="pm-tool-kicker">
          Carton volume analysis
        </p>

        <h1>
          See how much of the box's space
          your items require.
        </h1>

        <p>
          Calculate a volume-only utilization estimate
          for identical rectangular items against one
          carton's internal dimensions. This does not
          generate or verify a packing arrangement.
        </p>
      </header>

      <BoxUtilizationCalculator client:load />

      <div class="pm-utilization-copy">
        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">How it works</p>
          <h2>Volume used ÷ internal carton volume.</h2>
          <p>
            Packmetry multiplies each item's length, width
            and height, multiplies that volume by the item
            quantity, and divides by internal carton volume.
            Unallocated volume is carton volume minus
            requested item volume when the difference is
            nonnegative. Results use canonical unit
            conversions rather than mixing units.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Worked example</p>
          <h2>Six small items in a larger carton.</h2>
          <p>
            A 30 × 20 × 10 cm carton has 6,000 cm³ of
            internal volume. Six items measuring
            10 × 10 × 5 cm each request 3,000 cm³.
            That gives <strong>50% volume utilization</strong>
            and 3,000 cm³ of unallocated volume. These
            numbers do not establish that all six items
            can be placed without overlap.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Important boundary</p>
          <h2>Enough volume does not guarantee a fit.</h2>
          <p>
            Items with a combined volume below 100% can
            still fail to fit because of their dimensions,
            orientations or other constraints. If requested
            item volume exceeds 100%, all the items cannot
            fit within that carton. This calculator does
            not check rotations, positions, padding,
            spacing or stackability.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Canonical results</p>
          <h2>Verified utilization is a different result.</h2>
          <p>
            The <a href="/methodology/packing-algorithm/">
              packing methodology
            </a> explains how Packmetry measures actual
            utilization from independently verified
            placements. This standalone calculator instead
            estimates requested volume before a packing
            plan exists.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Next step</p>
          <h2>Need a real packing arrangement?</h2>
          <p>
            Use the <a href="/personal/">Personal workspace</a>
            to pack multiple items. For carton inventories,
            costs and weight constraints, use the
            <a href="/business/">Business workspace</a>.
            If you only need the minimum internal dimensions
            for one item, try the
            <a href="/tools/box-size-calculator/">
              box size calculator
            </a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
